<bp:ProteinReference rdf:about="http://identifiers.org/uniprot/P22304">
 <bp:entityFeature rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ModificationFeature_c1cae408a24ce32707f709944069be59" />
 <bp:standardName rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Iduronate 2-sulfatase</bp:standardName>
 <bp:xref rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/RelationshipXref_hgnc_symbol_IDS_identity" />
 <bp:xref rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/UnificationXref_uniprot_knowledgebase_P22304" />
 <bp:organism rdf:resource="http://identifiers.org/taxonomy/9606" />
 <bp:displayName rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">IDS_HUMAN</bp:displayName>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">SIDS</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Iduronate 2-sulfatase 14 kDa chain</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Idursulfase</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">IDS</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Iduronate 2-sulfatase 42 kDa chain</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">3.1.6.13</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Alpha-L-iduronate sulfate sulfatase</bp:name>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">IDS_HUMAN Reviewed; 550 AA.</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://identifiers.org/uniprot/Q5S2C4</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ProteinReference_uniprotkb_P22304_identity</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://mirtarbase.mbc.nctu.edu.tw/#ref_3423</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://identifiers.org/refseq/XP_006256465</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://identifiers.org/uniprot.isoform/P22304-3</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">FUNCTION: Required for the lysosomal degradation of heparansulfate and dermatan sulfate.CATALYTIC ACTIVITY:Reaction=Hydrolysis of the 2-sulfate groups of the L-iduronate 2-sulfate units of dermatan sulfate, heparan sulfate and heparin.;EC=3.1.6.13;COFACTOR:Name=Ca(2+); Xref=ChEBI:CHEBI:29108; Evidence={ECO:0000250};Note=Binds 1 Ca(2+) ion per subunit. {ECO:0000250};SUBUNIT: Liver iduronate 2-sulfatase is composed of two majorforms (A and B) which contain both a 42 kDa and a 14 kDapolypeptides.SUBCELLULAR LOCATION: Lysosome.ALTERNATIVE PRODUCTS:Event=Alternative splicing; Named isoforms=3;Name=1; Synonyms=Long;IsoId=P22304-1; Sequence=Displayed;Name=2; Synonyms=Short;IsoId=P22304-2; Sequence=VSP_006301, VSP_006302;Name=3;IsoId=P22304-3; Sequence=VSP_039116, VSP_039117;Note=No experimental confirmation available.;TISSUE SPECIFICITY: Liver, kidney, lung, and placenta.PTM: The conversion to 3-oxoalanine (also known as C-formylglycine, FGly), of a serine or cysteine residue inprokaryotes and of a cysteine residue in eukaryotes, is criticalfor catalytic activity. {ECO:0000250}.DISEASE: Mucopolysaccharidosis 2 (MPS2) [MIM:309900]: An X-linkedlysosomal storage disease characterized by intracellularaccumulation of heparan sulfate and dermatan sulfate and theirexcretion in urine. Most children with MPS2 have a severe formwith early somatic abnormalities including skeletal deformities,hepatosplenomegaly, and progressive cardiopulmonary deterioration.A prominent feature is neurological damage that presents asdevelopmental delay and hyperactivity but progresses to mentalretardation and dementia. They die before 15 years of age, usuallyas a result of obstructive airway disease or cardiac failure. Incontrast, those with a mild form of MPS2 may survive intoadulthood, with attenuated somatic complications and often withoutmental retardation. {ECO:0000269|PubMed:10215411,ECO:0000269|PubMed:10220152, ECO:0000269|PubMed:10447264,ECO:0000269|PubMed:10671065, ECO:0000269|PubMed:10838181,ECO:0000269|PubMed:11015461, ECO:0000269|PubMed:11683780,ECO:0000269|PubMed:11731225, ECO:0000269|PubMed:12655569,ECO:0000269|PubMed:12794697, ECO:0000269|PubMed:1284597,ECO:0000269|PubMed:1303211, ECO:0000269|PubMed:16699754,ECO:0000269|PubMed:7581397, ECO:0000269|PubMed:7599640,ECO:0000269|PubMed:7728156, ECO:0000269|PubMed:7866405,ECO:0000269|PubMed:7887413, ECO:0000269|PubMed:7981716,ECO:0000269|PubMed:8281149, ECO:0000269|PubMed:8566953,ECO:0000269|PubMed:8664909, ECO:0000269|PubMed:8830188,ECO:0000269|PubMed:8940265, ECO:0000269|PubMed:9222763,ECO:0000269|PubMed:9266380, ECO:0000269|PubMed:9375851,ECO:0000269|PubMed:9452044, ECO:0000269|PubMed:9501270,ECO:0000269|PubMed:9660053, ECO:0000269|PubMed:9762601,ECO:0000269|PubMed:9875019, ECO:0000269|PubMed:9921913,ECO:0000269|PubMed:9950361}. Note=The disease is caused bymutations affecting the gene represented in this entry.SIMILARITY: Belongs to the sulfatase family. {ECO:0000305}. COPYRIGHT: UniProt Consortium (www.uniprot.org). Distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution-NoDerivs License.</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://identifiers.org/uniprot/B4DGD7</bp:comment>
</bp:ProteinReference>

