 <bp:entityFeature rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ModificationFeature_ebd11a1cc3c10ba3fcbfb590acfa5bc1" />
 <bp:standardName rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">N-sulphoglucosamine sulphohydrolase</bp:standardName>
 <bp:xref rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/RelationshipXref_hgnc_symbol_SGSH_identity" />
 <bp:xref rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/UnificationXref_uniprot_knowledgebase_P51688" />
 <bp:organism rdf:resource="http://identifiers.org/taxonomy/9606" />
 <bp:displayName rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">SPHM_HUMAN</bp:displayName>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">3.10.1.1</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Sulphamidase</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">HSS</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Sulfoglucosamine sulfamidase</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">SGSH</bp:name>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://mirtarbase.mbc.nctu.edu.tw/#ref_6448</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">SPHM_HUMAN Reviewed; 502 AA.</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ProteinReference_uniprotkb_P51688_identity</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://biocyc.org/biopax/biopax-level3ProteinReference105487</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://identifiers.org/refseq/NP_000190</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">FUNCTION: Catalyzes a step in lysosomal heparan sulfatedegradation. {ECO:0000269|PubMed:15146460,ECO:0000269|PubMed:24816101, ECO:0000269|PubMed:7493035}.CATALYTIC ACTIVITY:Reaction=H2O + N-sulfo-D-glucosamine = D-glucosamine + sulfate;Xref=Rhea:RHEA:17881, ChEBI:CHEBI:15377, ChEBI:CHEBI:16189,ChEBI:CHEBI:57868, ChEBI:CHEBI:58723; EC=3.10.1.1;Evidence={ECO:0000269|PubMed:15146460,ECO:0000269|PubMed:24816101, ECO:0000269|PubMed:7493035};COFACTOR:Name=Ca(2+); Xref=ChEBI:CHEBI:29108;Evidence={ECO:0000269|PubMed:24816101};Note=Binds 1 Ca(2+) ion per subunit.{ECO:0000269|PubMed:24816101};SUBCELLULAR LOCATION: Lysosome {ECO:0000269|PubMed:15146460}.PTM: The conversion to 3-oxoalanine (also known as C-formylglycine, FGly), of a serine or cysteine residue inprokaryotes and of a cysteine residue in eukaryotes, is criticalfor catalytic activity. {ECO:0000269|PubMed:24816101}.DISEASE: Mucopolysaccharidosis 3A (MPS3A) [MIM:252900]: A severeform of mucopolysaccharidosis type 3, an autosomal recessivelysosomal storage disease due to impaired degradation of heparansulfate. MPS3 is characterized by severe central nervous systemdegeneration, but only mild somatic disease. Onset of clinicalfeatures usually occurs between 2 and 6 years; severe neurologicdegeneration occurs in most patients between 6 and 10 years ofage, and death occurs typically during the second or third decadeof life. MPS3A is characterized by earlier onset, rapidprogression of symptoms and shorter survival.{ECO:0000269|PubMed:11182930, ECO:0000269|PubMed:11793481,ECO:0000269|PubMed:12000360, ECO:0000269|PubMed:12702166,ECO:0000269|PubMed:15146460, ECO:0000269|PubMed:15637719,ECO:0000269|PubMed:15902564, ECO:0000269|PubMed:16311287,ECO:0000269|PubMed:17128482, ECO:0000269|PubMed:18407553,ECO:0000269|PubMed:21671382, ECO:0000269|PubMed:28101780,ECO:0000269|PubMed:9158154, ECO:0000269|PubMed:9285796,ECO:0000269|PubMed:9401012, ECO:0000269|PubMed:9554748,ECO:0000269|PubMed:9744479}. Note=The disease is caused bymutations affecting the gene represented in this entry.SIMILARITY: Belongs to the sulfatase family. {ECO:0000305}. COPYRIGHT: UniProt Consortium (www.uniprot.org). Distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution-NoDerivs License.</bp:comment>
</bp:ProteinReference>

